  "meta": {
    "title": "Coffee counter order call sequence",
    "question": "Who calls whom, in what order, from order submission through payment branches into asynchronous fulfillment and the ready-notification model — including the planned plain-text export?",
    "scope": "The seven JavaScript modules under sample-repo read as one call sequence (four synchronous order modules plus the fulfillment queue, runner, and counter notifier); the counter caller itself lives outside the sample repository.",
    "languageProfile": "javascript-esm",
    "uiLocale": "en",
    "diagramKind": "sequence",
    "summary": "The sequence companion to the flowchart demo: the same sample repository read as a call order, in two phases. Synchronously, the counter caller (inferred — the caller lives outside the repository) hands the order to the order service, which normalizes items; the rest of the synchronous chain lives inside the reservation-succeeded condition because a failed reservation or a rejected charge propagates the error straight to the caller — within it, the charge splits into exclusive accepted/guard-error branches, and only an accepted charge reaches the receipt step and the asynchronous hand-off to the fulfillment queue (inferred wiring). Asynchronously, a runner claims the job only when one is queued, honors the three-attempt limit, calls the notifier to build the counter-display notification model, and marks the job fulfilled, reporting the outcome back. Every verified claim cites inspected lines of the sample repository; inferred claims mark wiring the repository implies but does not contain, and the planned plain-text export stays unconfirmed. Guard calls are drawn without activation bars because a thrown error ends the exchange on that path."
  },
